rationale:
Apply the negative exponent by inverting the fraction and changing the exponent sign to positive:
$$\left(\frac{z^4}{2x^3y^{-2}}\right)^2 = \frac{z^8}{4x^6y^{-4}} = \frac{z^8y^4}{4x^6}

 $$

questionText:
For what value of $k$ will the system of linear equations have infinitely many solutions?
$$\begin{cases} 3x - 2y = 7 \\ 9x - 6y = 2k + 1 \end{cases}

$$

Find all real solutions $(x, y)$ to the system of equations:
$$\begin{cases} x^2 + y^2 = 25 \\ x - y = 1 \end{cases}

$$

Find the value of the following telescoping infinite series sum:
$$\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^2 + 3n + 2}

 $$
